{"task_id": "HumanEval/33", "solution": "def sort_third(l: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the indicies that are not divisible by three, while its values at the indicies that are divisible by three are equal\n    to the values of the corresponding indicies of l, but sorted.\n    >>> sort_third([1, 2, 3])\n    [1, 2, 3]\n    >>> sort_third([5, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 2])\n    [2, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 5]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not l:\n        return []\n\n    divisible_by_3_values = []\n    divisible_by_3_indices = []\n\n    # Extract values and their original indices that are divisible by three.\n    for i in range(len(l)):\n        if i % 3 == 0:\n            divisible_by_3_values.append(l[i])\n            divisible_by_3_indices.append(i)\n\n    # Sort the extracted values.\n    divisible_by_3_values.sort()\n\n    # Create a new list to store the result, copying all elements initially.\n    result = list(l)\n\n    # Place the sorted values back into the result list at their original divisible-by-three indices.\n    for j in range(len(divisible_by_3_indices)):\n        original_index = divisible_by_3_indices[j]\n        result[original_index] = divisible_by_3_values[j]\n\n    return result"}
